@page "/palette"

<CatHead Title="Command palette">
    The <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> box: type, see matching commands, run one. A
    <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, so the top layer, the focus trap and <kbd>Escape</kbd> are the
    platform's.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Palette.CommandPalette" Title="Open it" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> This site registers one command per page with
        <code>sednaUi.palette.register</code>, and writes the palette itself in its layout — the
        markup below. Until something is registered, or with no palette in the page, the library
        leaves the browser's own <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> alone. A command registered from C# carries an
        <code>Href</code> and navigates. A <code>run</code> callback is a JavaScript function, and a
        function does not cross into C# — navigation is what a serialisable command carries.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Palette/RegisterCommands.txt" Title="Registering commands">
    <p>
        <code>register()</code> replaces the whole list, so call it again whenever what is
        available changes — after a permission check, or on navigation. <code>keywords</code> makes
        a command findable by a word that is not in its label.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.PaletteFromCsharp" Title="Its ranking, from C#" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; type &ldquo;drawer&rdquo;.</strong> <code>RankCommandsAsync</code> ranks the registered
        commands against a query with the palette's own matcher and answers them best first &mdash; an empty query
        answers every one in registration order &mdash; for an app that shows the same ranking in a list of its own:
        the start of a page, an empty state. <code>OpenPaletteAsync</code> opens the palette itself, and
        <code>ClosePaletteAsync</code> closes it, which a layout does after a navigation the palette did not
        start; <a href="/interop">From C#</a> has that layout.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Palette.PaletteStates" Title="Matching, and nothing found" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The two states side by side, laid out statically so both are visible at once. The panel is
        <code>.palette</code>'s wherever its markup is, so a <code>&lt;div class="palette"&gt;</code>
        draws the same box as the dialog &mdash; for a picture of the palette in help text, or commands
        kept on screen &mdash; and only the place near the top and the backdrop belong to
        <code>&lt;dialog class="palette"&gt;</code>. Here with
        <code>.palette-footer</code> carrying the keys as <code>.kbd</code> literals.
        <code>.palette-group</code> heads a run of commands from the same group and is dropped once
        a query has reordered the list; its <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> needs
        <code>role="presentation"</code>, because a <code>listbox</code> may own only
        <code>option</code>s and a bare <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> breaks
        <code>aria-required-children</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Palette.PalettePages" Title="Pages, separators and loading" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A command with <code>opens</code> opens a page of the commands registered with the same
        <code>page</code>, instead of running &mdash; &ldquo;Change theme&hellip;&rdquo; and the themes.
        <kbd>Backspace</kbd> in an empty search, <kbd>Escape</kbd> and the app's
        <code>data-palette-back</code> button go back one page, to the query and the row the reader left;
        <kbd>Escape</kbd> on the root closes. The script shows <code>.palette-path</code> on a page, hides it on
        the root, and fills <code>data-palette-trail</code> with one <code>.palette-crumb</code> per page
        opened. With nothing typed, <code>.palette-separator</code> goes between two groups; a query drops
        both. While the dialog has <code>aria-busy="true"</code>, the list ends with the app's
        <code>.palette-loading</code> row and &ldquo;nothing matches&rdquo; is held back. A
        <code>pinned</code> command follows the matches whatever is typed. The arrow keys wrap. Laid out
        statically here, with every state at once.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Interop/PalettePages.txt" Title="Pages from C#">
    <p>
        <code>PaletteCommand.Opens</code>, <code>Page</code> and <code>Pinned</code> carry the same three
        things across the interop boundary. <code>RankCommandsAsync</code> ranks the root page.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Palette/PaletteMarkup.html" Title="The markup">
    <p>
        What the library builds, for an app rendering its own command list — if you are calling
        <code>register()</code> you write none of it. The list is a real
        <code>role="listbox"</code> of <code>role="option"</code> items with the input as the
        combobox owning it through <code>aria-activedescendant</code>,
        <code>aria-selected</code> is the keyboard's position and not <code>:hover</code>, and
        <code>.palette-empty</code> should name what was searched rather than say &ldquo;no
        results&rdquo;; the ranking is <code>sednaUi._.score</code>, shared with the
        <a href="/topbar">header search</a> so the two cannot rank one query differently.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>
